WEBVTT
X-TIMESTAMP-MAP=LOCAL:00:00:00.000,MPEGTS:127826

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.202
In this lesson, we'll go through a problem

2
00:00:02.202 --> 00:00:03.770
that doesn't get enough attention.

3
00:00:03.770 --> 00:00:06.773
Deploying agents to production is surprisingly hard.

4
00:00:06.873 --> 00:00:10.677
You've built an agent, maybe you used LangGraph, BeeAI,

5
00:00:10.677 --> 00:00:13.780
Google's agent development kit or your own custom code.

6
00:00:13.780 --> 00:00:15.281
And it works great on your

7
00:00:15.281 --> 00:00:17.050
laptop, but now you need to actually

8
00:00:17.050 --> 00:00:19.652
deploy it so your organization can use it.

9
00:00:19.953 --> 00:00:21.855
In this lesson, we're going to take all

10
00:00:21.855 --> 00:00:24.024
of the A2A agents you've built in previous lessons

11
00:00:24.124 --> 00:00:26.326
and deploy them onto Agent Stack,

12
00:00:26.426 --> 00:00:28.628
IBM Research's open-source platform

13
00:00:28.728 --> 00:00:33.728
that provides self-hostable infrastructure for sharing your agents. Let's code and innovate.

14
00:00:34.100 --> 00:00:35.835
When you decide to take your

15
00:00:35.835 --> 00:00:37.904
agent from locally running on your device

16
00:00:37.904 --> 00:00:40.106
to something that can be ran remotely,

17
00:00:40.306 --> 00:00:43.710
suddenly you're dealing with a whole list of infrastructure to-dos.

18
00:00:43.910 --> 00:00:47.180
You need persistent storage for session and conversation history.

19
00:00:47.180 --> 00:00:50.884
You need to configure the LLM APIs and manage those connections.

20
00:00:51.418 --> 00:00:54.921
If you're doing RAG, you need file storage and vector search,

21
00:00:55.321 --> 00:00:57.323
and then there's the deployment layer,

22
00:00:57.424 --> 00:01:02.128
which requires containers, scaling, monitoring, logging, and more.

23
00:01:02.629 --> 00:01:05.632
And you can't forget security, where you'll handle rate limiting,

24
00:01:05.932 --> 00:01:07.734
authentication, and access controls.

25
00:01:08.301 --> 00:01:11.071
Oh, and you'll need a way for end users

26
00:01:11.071 --> 00:01:13.673
to interact with your deployed agents through a UI.

27
00:01:14.374 --> 00:01:16.276
There's a lot that goes into a

28
00:01:16.276 --> 00:01:18.178
full platform. So what are your options?

29
00:01:18.378 --> 00:01:20.480
Well, there are framework-specific platforms

30
00:01:20.480 --> 00:01:23.383
like LangGraph Cloud or CrewAI's platform.

31
00:01:23.683 --> 00:01:25.985
And these are self-hostable, which is great.

32
00:01:26.286 --> 00:01:28.288
But you're locked into that one framework.

33
00:01:28.655 --> 00:01:31.157
If your team wants to experiment with different approaches

34
00:01:31.257 --> 00:01:35.962
or you're managing multiple projects that use different frameworks, that's not supported.

35
00:01:36.262 --> 00:01:38.064
Then there are cloud vendor platforms,

36
00:01:38.364 --> 00:01:41.468
like Azure AI, Vertex AI, AWS Bedrock,

37
00:01:41.868 --> 00:01:43.670
and Vercel AI. And these are

38
00:01:43.703 --> 00:01:46.206
framework agnostic, which solves that problem.

39
00:01:46.506 --> 00:01:48.808
But now you're committed to a specific cloud vendor

40
00:01:49.109 --> 00:01:51.511
and locked into specific parts of the stack,

41
00:01:51.811 --> 00:01:53.613
which means you lose full flexibility

42
00:01:53.813 --> 00:01:55.415
if you want to change your setup.

43
00:01:55.615 --> 00:01:58.318
Additionally, depending on your industry and use case,

44
00:01:58.718 --> 00:02:00.820
platforms just aren't an option

45
00:02:00.820 --> 00:02:03.223
because data can't leave your infrastructure

46
00:02:03.523 --> 00:02:05.925
and it needs to stay on your own servers.

47
00:02:06.226 --> 00:02:09.229
So the only option remains to build it yourself,

48
00:02:09.529 --> 00:02:11.731
which takes months of infrastructure work

49
00:02:11.731 --> 00:02:13.333
before you even get to deploy your

50
00:02:13.333 --> 00:02:15.135
first agent, or you can use Agent Stack.

51
00:02:15.168 --> 00:02:18.972
Agent Stack is an Open-Source Linux Foundation project

52
00:02:18.972 --> 00:02:21.975
by IBM Research that provides self-hostable infrastructure

53
00:02:21.975 --> 00:02:24.344
for your agents that's optimized for speed,

54
00:02:24.344 --> 00:02:26.946
flexibility, and control.

55
00:02:26.946 --> 00:02:28.948
It lets you deploy any agent,

56
00:02:28.948 --> 00:02:31.551
regardless of what framework you built it with.

57
00:02:31.584 --> 00:02:35.088
to production in hours instead of weeks or months.

58
00:02:35.388 --> 00:02:38.391
Because it's infrastructure, you only need to adopt the components

59
00:02:38.491 --> 00:02:41.494
of the platform that suit you and your company's needs.

60
00:02:41.594 --> 00:02:43.997
And feel free to build the rest as you see fit.

61
00:02:44.063 --> 00:02:46.366
It's designed for two types of teams.

62
00:02:46.666 --> 00:02:48.368
First, agent development teams

63
00:02:48.568 --> 00:02:50.336
who are exploring multiple frameworks

64
00:02:50.436 --> 00:02:52.939
and need real infrastructure that doesn't lock them in.

65
00:02:53.039 --> 00:02:55.542
Hence why every agent uses the A2A protocol

66
00:02:55.642 --> 00:02:59.345
to standardize communication. And second, platform teams

67
00:02:59.546 --> 00:03:01.848
who are managing multiple internal projects

68
00:03:01.948 --> 00:03:05.752
and need one unified system for all their deployments. Both of these

69
00:03:05.752 --> 00:03:08.955
teams need the same things: control over their data,

70
00:03:09.055 --> 00:03:12.559
flexibility to use any framework, and the ability to deploy fast.

71
00:03:12.659 --> 00:03:14.260
That's what Agent Stack gives you.

72
00:03:14.360 --> 00:03:17.363
No vendor lock-in, no months of infrastructure work,

73
00:03:17.463 --> 00:03:20.533
just straightforward production-ready deployment for AI agents.

74
00:03:20.633 --> 00:03:23.536
Now let's install it together in just a couple short commands.

75
00:03:23.636 --> 00:03:26.940
You'll only need one line to install Agent Stack through the CLI.

76
00:03:30.043 --> 00:03:32.045
Now we're going to go through the setup process.

77
00:03:32.145 --> 00:03:33.580
So it's asking me, do I want

78
00:03:33.580 --> 00:03:35.014
to start the Agent Stack platform now?

79
00:03:35.315 --> 00:03:37.317
Yes.

80
00:03:45.892 --> 00:03:49.195
Now it's asking us if we want to configure our LLM provider.

81
00:03:49.495 --> 00:03:51.497
And we do. So we're going to hit yes.

82
00:03:51.864 --> 00:03:54.367
Now it's asking us to choose our API key.

83
00:03:55.268 --> 00:03:57.570
I'm going to choose Google Gemini for this course.

84
00:03:59.872 --> 00:04:01.841
We enter our API key.

85
00:04:04.377 --> 00:04:07.180
Now it's asking us to choose the default model.

86
00:04:08.181 --> 00:04:10.883
I'm going to hit no because we're going to use

87
00:04:10.883 --> 00:04:12.885
a lighter weight model for this exercise.

88
00:04:13.786 --> 00:04:15.388
So we're going to use

89
00:04:16.389 --> 00:04:18.891
gemini-2.5-flash-

90
00:04:20.393 --> 00:04:24.397
lite.

91
00:04:24.897 --> 00:04:28.401
And we are going to use the recommended embedding model.

92
00:04:28.501 --> 00:04:30.603
And we're all set.

93
00:04:30.603 --> 00:04:34.107
Anytime you want to change your model provider,

94
00:04:34.107 --> 00:04:36.109
you can run agentstack model setup.

95
00:04:36.609 --> 00:04:39.112
Inside Agent Stack, you have three layers.

96
00:04:39.412 --> 00:04:42.115
At the core of the stack is the Agent Stack Server,

97
00:04:42.115 --> 00:04:45.618
which is a self-hostable server that can be deployed via Helm Charts

98
00:04:45.618 --> 00:04:48.621
and provides your agents with a scalable runtime.

99
00:04:49.122 --> 00:04:52.625
Then we have the top-level components like the Agent Stack Server SDK,

100
00:04:52.625 --> 00:04:55.128
which we'll use in this lesson

101
00:04:55.128 --> 00:04:57.597
to get agents ready to deploy onto the Agent Stack.

102
00:04:58.097 --> 00:04:59.966
We have a CLI to deploy, configure,

103
00:04:59.966 --> 00:05:01.801
and manage your agents on the platform,

104
00:05:01.801 --> 00:05:03.803
and both auto-generated UI extensions to get

105
00:05:03.803 --> 00:05:06.105
your agent in front of others quickly

106
00:05:06.139 --> 00:05:09.342
or a client SDK for custom-built UIs.

107
00:05:09.642 --> 00:05:12.645
Optionally, you can take advantage of our infrastructure services,

108
00:05:12.945 --> 00:05:16.349
such as LLM Provider Management, RAG capabilities,

109
00:05:16.649 --> 00:05:20.953
File Storage, a Data Layer, Authentication, and Secret Management.

110
00:05:21.354 --> 00:05:23.556
On Agent Stack, each agent that

111
00:05:23.556 --> 00:05:26.159
is deployed is implicitly an A2A agent,

112
00:05:26.359 --> 00:05:28.361
because the Agent Stack SDK server

113
00:05:28.661 --> 00:05:30.963
is built on top of the agent-to-agent protocol.

114
00:05:31.164 --> 00:05:33.366
You can learn more about the Agent Stack SDK

115
00:05:33.666 --> 00:05:36.069
in the resources available at the end of the lesson.

116
00:05:36.135 --> 00:05:39.706
Now we're going to move on to the AgentStack-HealthcareAgent repo.

117
00:05:39.906 --> 00:05:41.974
This is a healthcare-based example of how

118
00:05:41.974 --> 00:05:44.010
to build and deploy an A2A Agent

119
00:05:44.010 --> 00:05:46.045
that calls other A2A Agents onto

120
00:05:46.045 --> 00:05:48.414
an open source platform called Agent Stack.

121
00:05:48.715 --> 00:05:50.783
It's a public repo so you can take a look

122
00:05:50.783 --> 00:05:52.819
at it and go through the code at any time.

123
00:05:53.119 --> 00:05:54.887
But we're going to review some of the

124
00:05:54.887 --> 00:05:56.622
agents that we built in the previous lessons

125
00:05:56.622 --> 00:05:58.624
and see how to deploy them onto Agent Stack.

126
00:05:58.725 --> 00:06:00.493
I'll walk through transforming two of

127
00:06:00.493 --> 00:06:02.595
the agents that we created, the provider

128
00:06:02.595 --> 00:06:06.566
agent and the healthcare agent. You'll learn how the Agent Stack platform

129
00:06:06.666 --> 00:06:09.469
supplies the server wrapper that makes it an A2A agent.

130
00:06:10.169 --> 00:06:12.872
The LLM provisioning, the UI if desired,

131
00:06:13.473 --> 00:06:15.308
and the handoff between agents. You

132
00:06:15.308 --> 00:06:17.477
don't need to rewrite the agent logic.

133
00:06:17.977 --> 00:06:20.079
Instead, you're adding the platform plumbing.

134
00:06:20.380 --> 00:06:23.783
Let's start with the provider agent in the provider_agent folder.

135
00:06:24.183 --> 00:06:28.688
Then into the agentstack_agents, and finally, we have our provider_agent.py file.

136
00:06:29.055 --> 00:06:31.257
We're not going to read through every line,

137
00:06:31.257 --> 00:06:32.759
as these are the same agents

138
00:06:32.759 --> 00:06:34.527
that we built in the previous lessons.

139
00:06:34.527 --> 00:06:37.397
But I am going to point out the parts that are

140
00:06:37.397 --> 00:06:40.233
necessary to bring the agent on to the Agent Stack platform.

141
00:06:40.233 --> 00:06:43.536
So first we have our ProviderAgent class.

142
00:06:43.536 --> 00:06:45.705
This is the same agent logic as

143
00:06:45.705 --> 00:06:47.840
we created it in the previous lesson.

144
00:06:53.312 --> 00:06:56.516
Then we create an instance of the Agent Stack server.

145
00:06:56.716 --> 00:07:01.020
We decorate the entry point with the server.agent decorator,

146
00:07:01.320 --> 00:07:04.023
and we give it the name ProviderAgent.

147
00:07:04.724 --> 00:07:06.793
This binds the agent to the server

148
00:07:06.793 --> 00:07:09.495
and gives it a name which it can be discovered by

149
00:07:09.495 --> 00:07:11.197
on the Agent Stack platform.

150
00:07:11.798 --> 00:07:14.500
Since we want to use the LLM inference,

151
00:07:14.801 --> 00:07:18.604
the message input, and the context managed by the platform,

152
00:07:18.905 --> 00:07:21.207
we create a provider agent wrapper.

153
00:07:21.641 --> 00:07:26.446
This makes sure that the ProviderAgent uses the AgentStack extension functionality.

154
00:07:31.217 --> 00:07:33.853
We build our LangChain OpenAI client

155
00:07:33.853 --> 00:07:36.656
using the credentials from the platform rather

156
00:07:37.023 --> 00:07:39.225
than the local environment variables

157
00:07:39.225 --> 00:07:40.827
or secrets that we might be used to.

158
00:07:41.127 --> 00:07:44.230
Just like the input needed to be an AgentStack message object,

159
00:07:44.330 --> 00:07:47.233
so does the output in order for it to be properly returned

160
00:07:47.233 --> 00:07:48.935
to the AgentStack platform.

161
00:07:49.135 --> 00:07:52.038
So we yield the agent message as the agent response.

162
00:07:52.438 --> 00:07:54.607
Finally, we run the server

163
00:07:55.107 --> 00:07:57.910
and set the appropriate host and port.

164
00:07:58.211 --> 00:08:00.413
It's important to remember that we didn't

165
00:08:00.413 --> 00:08:02.615
change any of the base agent logic.

166
00:08:02.915 --> 00:08:04.917
The LangChain code remains the same.

167
00:08:05.218 --> 00:08:08.221
We just take advantage of the AgentStack SDK's extensions

168
00:08:08.621 --> 00:08:10.623
to easily turn the A2A agent

169
00:08:10.823 --> 00:08:12.925
into an AgentStack compatible agent.

170
00:08:13.359 --> 00:08:15.761
Now let's move on to the healthcare agent.

171
00:08:15.862 --> 00:08:18.664
We'll see the same basic platform setup,

172
00:08:18.865 --> 00:08:22.268
plus extra features that allow the user to interact effectively

173
00:08:22.268 --> 00:08:26.172
with the healthcare agent through an automatically generated UI.

174
00:08:30.643 --> 00:08:32.645
We have our imports.

175
00:08:35.882 --> 00:08:37.850
Then we instantiate our Server.

176
00:08:39.886 --> 00:08:41.888
We have some helper functions

177
00:08:41.921 --> 00:08:44.590
that allow us to keep session memory on the platform

178
00:08:44.590 --> 00:08:47.693
across agent handoffs and some other helper functions.

179
00:08:51.197 --> 00:08:54.200
Then again, we use our @server.agent decorator

180
00:08:54.200 --> 00:08:58.905
to bind the agent. This agent is named Healthcare Concierge,

181
00:08:59.205 --> 00:09:01.407
and we also set a bunch of extra parameters

182
00:09:01.407 --> 00:09:04.210
that gives the platform details about the agent

183
00:09:04.210 --> 00:09:06.612
like its input and output modalities,

184
00:09:07.113 --> 00:09:09.615
its AgentDetail, its UI greeting,

185
00:09:10.316 --> 00:09:12.618
contributors, tools, and more.

186
00:09:16.856 --> 00:09:20.560
Again, we're using the LLM inference managed by the platform.

187
00:09:21.060 --> 00:09:23.262
So we add the LLMServiceExtension.

188
00:09:23.663 --> 00:09:27.266
But you might also notice that we've added a TrajectoryExtension.

189
00:09:28.768 --> 00:09:30.536
This will help to show the agent

190
00:09:30.536 --> 00:09:32.271
trace as it works through a problem,

191
00:09:32.672 --> 00:09:34.307
and it gives the user better

192
00:09:34.307 --> 00:09:36.175
insights into what's happening behind the scenes.

193
00:09:45.952 --> 00:09:47.853
Now just like in the last lesson

194
00:09:47.954 --> 00:09:50.156
where we built our local healthcare agent,

195
00:09:50.256 --> 00:09:52.858
we're adding the PolicyAgent, ResearchAgent,

196
00:09:52.959 --> 00:09:55.061
and ProviderAgent as tools

197
00:09:55.161 --> 00:09:57.663
for the main orchestrating agent to hand off to.

198
00:09:58.631 --> 00:10:00.933
However, this time we're making that assumption

199
00:10:00.933 --> 00:10:02.935
that those agents have already been deployed

200
00:10:02.935 --> 00:10:04.837
and are managed by AgentStack.

201
00:10:05.137 --> 00:10:08.040
So we discover them by searching for them by name

202
00:10:08.140 --> 00:10:11.143
from the directory of active agents on AgentStack,

203
00:10:11.344 --> 00:10:13.646
and we wrap them as handoff tools.

204
00:10:16.215 --> 00:10:18.417
Now we build our RequirementAgent

205
00:10:18.718 --> 00:10:21.420
and we add the other agents as conditional requirements.

206
00:10:21.921 --> 00:10:23.923
In this case for demo purposes,

207
00:10:24.023 --> 00:10:26.826
we want to ensure that each agent is ran at least once

208
00:10:27.126 --> 00:10:30.830
to know that the main orchestrator agent, which is an A2A agent,

209
00:10:30.930 --> 00:10:35.134
can call other A2A agents managed and deployed on the platform.

210
00:10:39.639 --> 00:10:42.341
We stream our response, trajectory,

211
00:10:42.842 --> 00:10:47.842
and final answer to the UI. And finally,

212
00:10:49.448 --> 00:10:53.052
We start the Agent Stack server for the Healthcare Concierge

213
00:10:53.252 --> 00:10:55.554
and set the appropriate host and port.

214
00:10:55.955 --> 00:10:58.457
Some key takeaways we learned from this lesson

215
00:10:58.457 --> 00:11:02.061
is that in order to transform your agents built in any framework

216
00:11:02.261 --> 00:11:04.563
into one that will run on Agent Stack,

217
00:11:04.664 --> 00:11:06.465
you need to wrap the server

218
00:11:06.465 --> 00:11:08.567
and add the @server.agent decorator.

219
00:11:08.734 --> 00:11:12.605
Use your Agent Stack compatible message input and output classes.

220
00:11:12.838 --> 00:11:16.208
Use the LLM extension to build your LLM client

221
00:11:16.409 --> 00:11:19.245
rather than using local secrets or environment variables.

222
00:11:19.545 --> 00:11:23.949
And if stateful, load, store, and maintain your session history.

223
00:11:24.383 --> 00:11:27.586
Optionally, you can add trajectory for observability,

224
00:11:28.187 --> 00:11:31.891
and lastly configure a run() entry point to start the server.

225
00:11:32.291 --> 00:11:35.594
Remember that your core agent logic remains unchanged.

226
00:11:35.961 --> 00:11:38.731
Agent Stack just adds the hosting and wiring

227
00:11:38.731 --> 00:11:40.933
to make it run on a deployed platform

228
00:11:40.933 --> 00:11:43.736
so you can share them with your organization.

229
00:11:44.136 --> 00:11:47.039
In my public repo, each agent has a folder.

230
00:11:47.273 --> 00:11:49.108
And within that folder are a

231
00:11:49.108 --> 00:11:51.277
few important components for deploying our agent.

232
00:11:51.744 --> 00:11:54.380
We already walked through the agent logic itself

233
00:11:54.380 --> 00:11:56.482
in the agentstack_agents folder.

234
00:11:56.882 --> 00:11:59.285
But I'd also like to show you the toml file.

235
00:12:00.052 --> 00:12:02.354
You need to ensure that the toml file

236
00:12:02.354 --> 00:12:04.757
has a project.scripts section.

237
00:12:05.257 --> 00:12:07.059
This actually starts the server.

238
00:12:09.929 --> 00:12:11.964
And then we have a Dockerfile, which

239
00:12:11.964 --> 00:12:13.966
you can use to build your agents.

240
00:12:14.567 --> 00:12:18.370
In the resources section of this lesson, you'll find a repo

241
00:12:18.370 --> 00:12:21.574
that you can use as a starter template for deploying your agents.

242
00:12:21.974 --> 00:12:24.376
Once you have everything in the correct structure,

243
00:12:24.543 --> 00:12:27.146
You can create a new release in GitHub.

244
00:12:27.346 --> 00:12:30.149
This is optional, but it gives us version control.

245
00:12:30.449 --> 00:12:32.451
So you can update your agents if

246
00:12:32.451 --> 00:12:34.453
you have a newly released version available,

247
00:12:34.653 --> 00:12:36.756
or roll back to a previous version.

248
00:12:37.156 --> 00:12:39.458
Now that you have everything set up correctly,

249
00:12:39.859 --> 00:12:43.362
it only takes one command to deploy your agent to Agent Stack.

250
00:12:43.629 --> 00:12:47.533
And whether you're deploying your agents to your local instance of agentstack

251
00:12:48.033 --> 00:12:51.437
or your organization's hosted version, you would do so the same way.

252
00:12:51.937 --> 00:12:54.106
First, let's install the PolicyAgent.

253
00:12:54.840 --> 00:12:58.244
We can see that our PolicyAgent has been deployed onto the platform.

254
00:12:58.744 --> 00:13:00.646
Now let's deploy the ProviderAgent.

255
00:13:03.849 --> 00:13:05.451
Next up, the ResearchAgent.

256
